\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We compare three VLA architectures built on distinct backbones: OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024_openvla,karamcheti2024} (Llama-2-7B~\cite{touvron2023_llama2}, 32 layers, 32 attention heads), TracVLA~\cite{zheng2024tracevla} (Phi-3-mini~\cite{abdin2024phi3}, 32 layers, 32 heads), and NORA~\cite{TODO_nora} (Qwen2.5-VL-3B, 36 layers, 16 heads with grouped-query attention).
Each architecture is evaluated on four LIBERO~\cite{liu2023_libero} task suites (Goal, Spatial, Object, Long-Horizon; 10 tasks each), yielding a $3 \times 4$ matrix with 10 of 12 cells filled (NORA Object and Long-Horizon checkpoints are unavailable).
For each cell we compare a trained and an untrained checkpoint using three analyses: $R^2$ action probes at every layer (\S\ref{sec:probes}), L1 attention head profiling (\S\ref{sec:attention_analysis}), and counterfactual instruction perturbation.
A key asymmetry distinguishes the untrained baselines: OpenVLA's untrained checkpoint is the OXE-pretrained model~\cite{padalkar2023_oxe} (970K episodes, already a VLA), whereas TracVLA and NORA untrained checkpoints are pure vision-language models (Phi-3-Vision and Qwen2.5-VL) with no action training.
This asymmetry is critical for interpreting the R$^2$ collapse results below.
Cohen's $d$ quantifies trained counterfactual sensitivity (action MSE); cosine distance quantifies untrained sensitivity (hidden representations).
A global Bonferroni correction across all 63 statistical tests preserves all core findings (Appendix~\ref{sec:bonferroni_correction}).

\subsection{OpenVLA: Instruction Routing and Functional Sensitivity}
\label{sec:openvla_routing}

We first establish the core phenomena on OpenVLA before testing cross-architecture generalization.
Cross-instruction probes confirm architecture-expected token specialization: image\_mean stays at chance ($10\%$) across all 32 layers in all conditions, while trained readout achieves $100\%$ 10-way instruction classification from L1 through L31 (LOOCV-confirmed; Appendix~\ref{sec:dense_routing},~\ref{sec:loocv}).
Untrained readout achieves $100\%$ at L1 but decays to $82\%$ by L31, confirming the readout as the primary multimodal integration site.

L1 attention profiling reveals a three-condition gradient in instruction-dominant heads ($>50\%$ attention to instruction tokens): untrained $0/32$ ($11.7\%$ text attention), Llama-2 base $9/32$ ($36.5\%$), VLA-trained $24/32$ ($57.7\%$).
The full 32-layer profile shows qualitatively distinct patterns: Llama-2 base concentrates instruction attention at mid-depth (L9--L16: 15--22 heads), while VLA training shifts to an entry-exit bookend (L0--L1: 21--24 heads, L31: 18 heads, L2--L30: 0--11 heads; Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold_sensitivity}).
A fourth condition (LIBERO fine-tuned) preserves L1 entry concentration ($22/32$ heads) but loses L31 exit re-engagement ($0$ vs.\ $18$ heads), consistent with exit-layer attention serving multi-task flexibility (Appendix~\ref{app:finetuned_probe}).

Counterfactual instruction perturbation across 10 LIBERO-Goal tasks produces divergence ordered by token novelty ($\rho = 0.8$): empty ($d = 0.978$), wrong task ($0.924$), shuffled ($0.763$), paraphrased ($0.707$).
The effect replicates across LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks): empty $d = 0.942 > $ wrong $0.851 > $ shuffled $0.833 > $ paraphrased $0.806$ (all $d > 0.8$; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:counterfactual_spatial}).
Dose-response ablation of 4--32 instruction-dominant heads yields monotonic trajectory divergence ($d = 0.482 \to 0.639$; Appendix~\ref{sec:action_trajectory}), and task-dependent modulation shows instruction-discriminative tasks with $2.5\times$ larger $|\Delta R^2|$ ($p = 0.033$; Appendix~\ref{sec:task_complexity}).
$R^2$ action probes show training effects concentrated at the readout ($+0.069$, $p < 0.0001$) rather than image tokens ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:action_r2_threecond}).

\subsection{$R^2$ Exit Collapse Across Architectures}
\label{sec:r2_collapse}

We define \emph{$R^2$ exit collapse} as the drop in action-probe $R^2$ from peak layer to exit layer ($\Delta = R^2_\text{peak} - R^2_\text{exit}$), measuring how much action-relevant information is discarded at exit layers.
Table~\ref{tab:cross_arch_matrix} reports $\Delta$ for all architecture-suite cells.

TracVLA shows consistent collapse across all four suites: trained $\Delta = 0.138$--$0.294$ versus untrained $\Delta = 0.009$--$0.048$.
The largest effect appears on Object ($\Delta_\text{trained} = 0.294$, $\Delta_\text{untrained} = 0.009$, a $33\times$ ratio).
NORA shows the most extreme collapse: Goal trained $\Delta = 0.691$ (exit $R^2$ drops to $-0.004$), Spatial trained $\Delta = 0.302$, compared to untrained $\Delta = 0.037$--$0.044$.
Both TracVLA and NORA untrained checkpoints are pure VLMs with no action training, and all six VLM-to-VLA comparisons show collapse (6/6 cells).

OpenVLA presents a contrasting pattern. Only Object shows clear collapse ($\Delta_\text{trained} = 0.121$ vs.\ $\Delta_\text{untrained} = 0.076$); Goal ($0.016$ vs.\ $0.033$) and Long-Horizon ($0.004$ vs.\ $0.016$) show no collapse, with untrained $\Delta$ actually exceeding trained $\Delta$ in both cases.
The critical distinction is that OpenVLA's untrained checkpoint is already an OXE-pretrained VLA (970K episodes of action training), not a pure VLM.
Exit collapse thus emerges consistently when vision-language models are first fine-tuned into VLAs (6/6 cells, TracVLA + NORA) but does not intensify with further task-specific training of an already-pretrained VLA (1/3 OpenVLA cells).
This pattern reveals exit collapse as a one-time reorganization at the VLM-to-VLA transition rather than a cumulative effect of continued training.

